"HiFA: A High-Performance and Flexible Acceleration Framework for Large-Size Number Theoretic Transform (https://cs.newpaltz.edu/~lik/publications/Qilin-Hu-ACM-TRTS-2025)\nciteturn5view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 32; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://cs.newpaltz.edu/~lik/publications/Qilin-Hu-ACM-TRTS-2025\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1905\nL0@P0: HiFA: A High-Performance and Flexible Acceleration\nL1@P0: Framework for Large-Size Number Theoretic Transform\nL2@P0: QILIN HU, HAOTIAN WANG, and CHUBO LIU, College of Computer Science and Electronic\nL3@P0: Engineering, Hunan University, Changsha, China and the Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of “Fusion\nL4@P0: Computing of Supercomputing and Artificial Intelligence”, Changsha, China\nL5@P0: KEQIN LI, College of Computer Science and Electronic Engineering, Hunan University, Changsha, China\nL6@P0: and the Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of “Fusion Computing of Supercomputing and Artificial\nL7@P0: Intelligence”, Changsha, China and State University of New York, New Paltz, New York, USA\nL8@P0: KENLI LI, College of Computer Science and Electronic Engineering, Hunan University, Changsha, China\nL9@P0: and the Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of “Fusion Computing of Supercomputing and Artificial\nL10@P0: Intelligence”, Changsha, China\nL11@P0: Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKP) and Homomorphic Encryption (HE) are crucial for data privacy in applications\nL12@P0: like cloud, blockchain, and analytics. However, the real-world adoption often faces performance challenges,\nL13@P0: particularly in the execution of the Number Theoretic Transform (NTT) required for polynomial multipli\u0002cation involving sizes beyond 2\nL14@P0: 20 and large integer widths (e.g., 256 bits). FPGAs offer a promising platform\nL15@P0: for acceleration, but efficiently implementing large-size NTTs remains difficult due to the limited on-chip\nL16@P0: resources. The widely adopted four-step NTT method, used to relieve the need for large on-chip memory,\nL17@P0: introduces performance bottlenecks. Initially, the traditional dataflow NTT architecture may not fully exploit\nL18@P0: available compute capability, which hinders achieving peak performance. Furthermore, during the matrix\nL19@P0: transpose phase, the non-sequential access to external High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM) causes inefficiency.\nL20@P0: To address these challenges, we introduce HiFA, an FPGA-based automatic accelerator framework designed\nL21@P0: for high-performance and flexible large-size NTT computations. HiFA utilizes a stacked NTT architecture for\nL22@P0: high parallelism, maximizing HBM throughput. It supports various decomposed polynomial sizes via a novel\nL23@P0: reordering module. Additionally, a specialized cyclic shuffle module is integrated to optimize data movement\nL24@P0: during the matrix transpose step, alleviating random memory access delay. HiFA also provides an automatic\nL25@P0: This work was supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant Nos. 62441234 and 62502151), the\nL26@P0: China Postdoctoral Science Foundation (Grant Nos. 2024M750879 and BX20240111), and the Hunan Provincial Innovation\nL27@P0: Foundation for Postgraduate (Grant No. CX20240420).\nL28@P0: Authors’ Contact Information: Qilin Hu, College of Computer Science and Electronic Engineering, Hunan University,\nL29@P0: Changsha, China and the Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of “Fusion Computing of Supercomputing and Artificial\nL30@P0: Intelligence”, Changsha, China; e-mail: hql@hnu.edu.cn; Haotian Wang (corresponding author), College of Computer\nL31@P0: Science and Electronic Engineering, Hunan University, Changsha, China and the Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of\nL32@P0: “Fusion Computing of Supercomputing and Artificial Intelligence”, Changsha, China; e-mail: wanghaotian@hnu.edu.cn;\nL33@P0: Chubo Liu, College of Computer Science and Electronic Engineering, Hunan University, Changsha, China and the Ministry\nL34@P0: of Education Key Laboratory of “Fusion Computing of Supercomputing and Artificial Intelligence”, Changsha, China; e-mail:\nL35@P0: liuchubo@hnu.edu.cn; Keqin Li, College of Computer Science and Electronic Engineering, Hunan University, Changsha,\nL36@P0: China and the Ministry of Education Key Laboratory of “Fusion Computing of Supercomputing and Artificial Intelligence”,\nL37@P0: Changsha, China and State University of New York, New Paltz, New York, USA; e-mail: lik@newpaltz.edu; Kenli Li, College\nL38@P0: of Computer Science and Electronic Engineering, Hunan University, Changsha, China and the Ministry of Education Key\nL39@P0: Laboratory of “Fusion Computing of Supercomputing and Artificial Intelligence”, Changsha, China; e-mail: lkl@hnu.edu.cn.\nL40@P0: Permission to make digital or hard copies of all or part of this work for personal or classroom use is granted without fee\nL41@P0: provided that copies are not made or distributed for profit or commercial advantage and that copies bear this notice and the\nL42@P0: full citation on the first page. Copyrights for components of this work owned by others than the author(s) must be honored.\nL43@P0: Abstracting with credit is permitted. To copy otherwise, or republish, to post on servers or to redistribute to lists, requires\nL44@P0: prior specific permission and/or a fee. Request permissions from permissions@acm.org.\nL45@P0: © 2025 Copyright held by the owner/author(s). Publication rights licensed to ACM.\nL46@P0: ACM 1936-7414/2025/12-ART54\nL47@P0: https://doi.org/10.1145/3771769\nL48@P0-1: ACM Transactions on Reconfigurable Technology and Systems, Vol. 18, No. 4, Article 54. Publication date: December 2025.54:2 Q. Hu et al.\nL49@P1: Design Space Exploration (DSE) framework that identifies optimal four-step decomposition parameters and\nL50@P1: generates corresponding hardware configurations. Our experiments show that the FPGA implementation of\nL51@P1: HiFA achieves an average speedup of 2.97× and up to 7.25× improvement in latency over prior state-of-the-art\nL52@P1: FPGA solutions. Compared to prior GPU-based methods, HiFA achieves an average energy efficiency gain\nL53@P1: of 2.24×.\nL54@P1: CCS Concepts: • Hardware → Hardware accelerators; • Security and privacy → Public key encryption;\nL55@P1: • Computer systems organization → Data flow architectures; High-level language architectures;\nL56@P1: Additional Key Words and Phrases: Design space exploration, FPGA acceleration, homomorphic encryption\nL57@P1: (HE), number theoretic transform (NTT), zero-knowledge proofs (ZKP)\nL58@P1: ACM Reference format:\nL59@P1: Qilin Hu, Haotian Wang, Chubo Liu, Keqin Li, and Kenli Li. 2025. HiFA: A High-Performance and Flexible\nL60@P1: Acceleration Framework for Large-Size Number Theoretic Transform. ACM Trans. Reconfig. Technol. Syst. 18,\nL61@P1: 4, Article 54 (December 2025), 32 pages.\nL62@P1: https://doi.org/10.1145/3771769\nL63@P1: 1 Introduction\nL64@P1: With the rapid development of the Big Data era, the necessity of protecting data privacy has become\nL65@P1: increasingly critical. Zero-Knowledge Proofs (ZKP) and Fully Homomorphic Encryption\nL66@P1: (FHE) schemes have emerged as essential cryptographic techniques to address this demand [48].\nL67@P1: These approaches enable secure computation on encrypted data, ensuring confidentiality while\nL68@P1: maintaining functionality, thus attracting significant attention across industries such as finance,\nL69@P1: healthcare, and cloud computing [15, 21]. ZKP protocols allow a prover to convince a verifier of the\nL70@P1: validity of a statement without revealing the secret information [16]. Homomorphic Encryption\nL71@P1: (HE), particularly FHE [14], allows arbitrary computations to be performed directly on encrypted\nL72@P1: data (ciphertext), yielding the result of a decrypted calculation equivalent to the one computed\nL73@P1: on the original plaintext.\nL74@P1: Large-size polynomial multiplication is a major performance bottleneck and a core operation\nL75@P1: in modern cryptography [25, 45]. Hence, the practical deployment of ZKP and HE schemes is also\nL76@P1: often constrained by polynomial multiplication, necessitating hardware acceleration. The standard\nL77@P1: Number Theoretic Transform (NTT) algorithm, based on the Cooley–Tukey (CT) Fast Fourier\nL78@P1: Transform (FFT) adapted for finite fields, converts polynomial coefficients into a point-value rep\u0002resentation, enabling efficient multiplication, reducing the computation complexity from 𝑂(𝑁\nL79@P1: 2\nL80@P1: ) to\nL81@P1: 𝑂(𝑁 log 𝑁). While various hardware platforms can implement NTT, FPGA provides promising NTT\nL82@P1: acceleration by exploiting fine-grained parallelism and enabling customized, energy-efficient ar\u0002chitectures for the inherent structure and various parameters of NTT computations over CPU/GPU\nL83@P1: implementations [35, 39]. Furthermore, compared to ASICs, the reconfigurability of FPGAs provides\nL84@P1: design flexibility for different standards or parameters of cryptographic schemes [8].\nL85@P1: The ZKP scheme can involve polynomial size exceeding 2\nL86@P1: 20, and particularly, it requires compu\u0002tations over wide integers with bit-widths of several hundred bits, such as 256 bits. Also, a larger\nL87@P1: polynomial size will highly improve the security and support a deeper multiplicative depth in HE\nL88@P1: schemes. Such large-size parameters are driven by the need to support complex cryptographic\nL89@P1: constructs, including secure multi-party computation, verifiable computation in blockchain, and\nL90@P1: privacy-preserving machine learning in cloud environments. However, implementing large-size\nL91@P1: NTTs on hardware platforms like FPGA presents significant challenges due to the necessity of\nL92@P1: using available on-chip computing and storage resources efficiently.\nL93@P1: For a polynomial of size 𝑁, the standard NTT requires 𝑂(𝑁 log 𝑁) arithmetic operations and\nL94@P1: storage for 𝑁 coefficients and 𝑁\nL95@P1: 2\nL96@P1: twiddle factors. Different stages in the transform may share the\nL97@P1-2: ACM Transactions on Reconfigurable Technology and Systems, Vol. 18, No. 4, Article 54. Publication date: DecA High-Performance and Flexible Acceleration Framework for Large-Size NTT 54:3\nL98@P2: Core0 Core1 Core9 Core10 …\nL99@P2: 211-size NTT PE\nL100@P2: 221 NTT 211 NTT\nL101@P2: 210 NTT\nL102@P2: 2D decomposition\nL103@P2: Fig. 1. Decomposition structures in prior large-size NTT accelerators.\nL104@P2: same twiddle factors. While effective for smaller polynomials (e.g., 𝑁 ≤ 2\nL105@P2: 14), the standard NTT\nL106@P2: becomes impractical for large 𝑁 (e.g., 𝑁 ≥ 2\nL107@P2: 20) on FPGAs, as the on-chip memory (e.g., block\nL108@P2: RAM) is insufficient to store all coefficients and twiddle factors simultaneously. Moreover, the\nL109@P2: wide integer widths amplify the computational complexity, requiring extensive modular arithmetic\nL110@P2: operations that strain FPGA logic resources.\nL111@P2: To address these limitations, the four-step NTT algorithm has emerged as a widely adopted\nL112@P2: approach for large-size NTTs in FPGA and ASIC designs [10, 40, 46]. Unlike the standard NTT,\nL113@P2: which processes the entire polynomial in a Butterfly Unit (BU)-based kernel, the four-step NTT\nL114@P2: decomposes the transform into four phases: (1) a column-wise NTT on smaller sub-polynomials,\nL115@P2: (2) a coefficient multiplication and matrix transposition to reorder data, (3) a row-wise NTT, and (4)\nL116@P2: another matrix transpose. This decomposition significantly reduces the on-chip memory demand.\nL117@P2: By storing only a subset of coefficients and twiddle factors on-chip, the four-step NTT enables large\u0002size NTTs to be computed on resource-constrained platforms, with the remaining data residing\nL118@P2: in High-Bandwidth Memory (HBM) or DDR off-chip memory. However, the four-step NTT\nL119@P2: introduces extra overheads, including doubled NTT computations (row- and column-wise), extra\nL120@P2: modular multiplications, and non-sequential HBM accesses during the transpose phase. Therefore,\nL121@P2: we need to carefully architect the design and off-chip memory access strategy.\nL122@P2: As shown in Figure 1(a), prior accelerators PipeZK leverage the existing NTT dataflow architec\u0002ture to implement the Processing Element (PE) in four-step NTT and use 2D decomposition to\nL123@P2: decompose the large-size NTT (𝑁 = 𝑛1 × 𝑛2) into two equal or close numbers (e.g., 𝑁 = 2\nL124@P2: 21 into\nL125@P2: 𝑛1 = 2\nL126@P2: 10 and 𝑛2 = 211) [46]. However, based on our observation, equal decomposition may not\nL127@P2: always provide a better performance compared to an imbalanced decomposition (e.g., 𝑁 = 2\nL128@P2: 21 into\nL129@P2: 𝑛1 = 2\nL130@P2: 9\nL131@P2: and 𝑛2 = 2\nL132@P2: 12) under specific hardware deployment, thus necessitating a flexible automation\nL133@P2: framework to explore and generate the design based on the best decomposition. Moreover, because\nL134@P2: each NTT PE in the dataflow architecture consumes a substantial amount of resources, it is difficult\nL135@P2: to deploy multiple PEs in parallel, which in turn reduces the overall compute density. To improve\nL136@P2: parallelism, SAM [40] employs a multi-dimensional decomposition that lowers each PE’s resource\nL137@P2: utilization and enables more PEs to be instantiated concurrently, as illustrated in Figure 1(b).\nL138@P2: However, it introduces additional element-wise multiplications and off-chip memory accesses,\nL139@P2: and its dataflow architecture leads to inefficient bandwidth utilization. Therefore, we need a new\nL140@P2: NTT computation architecture that fully exploits HBM while avoiding the latency penalties of\nL141@P2: non-sequential off-chip accesses during the matrix-transpose phase.\nL142@P2: To overcome the above limitations, we propose HiFA, a high-performance and flexible acceleration\nL143@P2: framework for large-size NTT on HBM-equipped FPGAs. HiFA employs a stacked NTT architecture\nL144@P2: with a novel reordering module to support diverse polynomial sizes to maximize parallelism and\nL145@P2-3: ACM Transactions on Reconfigurable Technology and Systems, Vol. 18, No. 4, Article 54. Publication date: De54:4 Q. Hu et al.\nL146@P3: explore HBM throughput. It also designs a cyclic shuffle module to streamline data movement during\nL147@P3: the transpose phase, mitigating random memory access delays. Additionally, HiFA integrates an\nL148@P3: automated DSE framework to optimize four-step decomposition parameters and generate tailored\nL149@P3: hardware configurations. In summary, the contributions of HiFA are listed as follows:\nL150@P3: (1) HiFA supports an optimized NTT PE that provides four-step NTT execution of imbalanced\nL151@P3: decomposition while fully utilizing BUs. It also supports indivisible polynomial size in four\u0002step NTT and is equipped with a specific coefficient reorder module, which enables different\nL152@P3: polynomial size computation.\nL153@P3: (2) HiFA provides a cyclic shuffler to avoid the extra latency caused by the random off-chip\nL154@P3: memory access before matrix transposition based on the evaluation of the impact of random\nL155@P3: memory access for HBM-FPGAs.\nL156@P3: (3) HiFA provides a DSE framework for four-step NTT, which gives insight that the close equally\nL157@P3: decomposition may not always give the best performance and thus systematically explores\nL158@P3: the design space and generates the best NTT implementation.\nL159@P3: (4) Compared to state-of-the-art FPGA implementations, HiFA achieves an average 2.97× im\u0002provement in end-to-end latency and up to 7.25×. In addition, our architecture enhances\nL160@P3: FPGA routability and enables higher operating frequency. Compared to GPU-based methods,\nL161@P3: HiFA achieves an average energy efficiency gain of 2.24×.\nL162@P3: The remainder of this article is organized as follows: Section 2 reviews the standard and four-step\nL163@P3: NTT algorithms and outlines the key challenges and opportunities in accelerating large-size NTTs.\nL164@P3: Section 3 presents HiFA, describing its overall computation flow, the customized NTT PE, the\nL165@P3: cyclic shuffle module that mitigates random off-chip memory accesses, and the on-chip matrix\nL166@P3: transpose engine. Section 4 explains our end-to-end DSE workflow. Section 5 reports and analyzes\nL167@P3: the experimental results. Section 6 surveys related work on large-size NTT acceleration across\nL168@P3: different hardware platforms: CPUs, GPUs, FPGAs, and ASICs. Finally, Section 7 concludes the\nL169@P3: paper and discusses future research.\nL170@P3: 2 Background\nL171@P3: NTT can be regarded as a variant of the classical Discrete Fourier Transform within a finite field,\nL172@P3: such as the residue class ring of integers modulus 𝑝, denoted as Z𝑝 . It uses modular addition and\nL173@P3: multiplication to evaluate the polynomial at 𝑁th roots of unity in Z𝑝 , avoiding complex arithmetic.\nL174@P3: As a result, it eliminates floating-point errors during calculations, making it particularly suitable for\nL175@P3: cryptographic applications that require high precision [38, 40]. Despite the algorithmic advantage,\nL176@P3: NTT computations remain a performance bottleneck in many ZKP and HE workloads. PipeZK\nL177@P3: shows that the polynomial arithmetic portion of proof generation accounts for ∼30% of total proving\nL178@P3: time, whereas all other polynomial operations contribute less than 2% [46]. In HE schemes, encryp\u0002tion/decryption and homomorphic operations on ciphertexts inherently involve polynomial mul\u0002tiplications, making NTT performance critical, especially during procedures like bootstrapping [2].\nL179@P3: 2.1 Standard NTT vs. Four-Step NTT\nL180@P3: NTT algorithm can be realized into two ways: Decimation-in-Time (DIT) and Decimation-in\u0002Frequency (DIF). The DIT formulation is built based on CT butterflies, whereas the DIF formulation\nL181@P3: relies on Gentleman–Sande (GS) butterflies [19, 36]. For a given input coefficient pair (𝑎, 𝑏) and\nL182@P3: a twiddle factor 𝜔\nL183@P3: 𝑖\nL184@P3: , the CT and GS butterflies compute the coefficient pairs \b𝑎 + 𝑏 · 𝜔\nL185@P3: 𝑖\nL186@P3: , 𝑎 − 𝑏 · 𝜔\nL187@P3: 𝑖\nL188@P3: \nL189@P3: and \b𝑎 + 𝑏, (𝑎 − 𝑏) · 𝜔\nL190@P3: 𝑖\nL191@P3: \nL192@P3: , respectively.\nL193@P3: Analogous to the FFT, NTT is typically implemented as a radix-2 iterative algorithm. The input\nL194@P3: of length 𝑁 (usually a power of 2 number) is processed in log 𝑁 stages of BUs. The coefficients\nL195@P3-4: ACM Transactions on Reconfigurable Technology and Systems, Vol. 18, No. 4, Article 54. PublicatA High-Performance and Flexible Acceleration Framework for Large-Size NTT 54:5\nL196@P4: Fig. 2. 8-point NTT dataflow based on Cooley–Tukey butterfly unit.\nL197@P4: to be computed in the same BU are decided by a stride that is doubled in each stage, ensuring\nL198@P4: that after log 𝑁 stages, every coefficient has interacted with every other coefficient, producing the\nL199@P4: transformed output. Figure 2 illustrates a complete 8-point NTT butterfly flow with a bit-reverse\nL200@P4: input and normal output, where 2 BUs are put in vertical and 2 BUs are put in horizontal for a 2 × 2\nL201@P4: Butterfly Unit Group (BUG). By reusing the 2 × 2 BUG twice with a specific coefficient shuffler\nL202@P4: in between, we can compute an 8-point NTT. In this article, we refer to this reuse of the BUG as\nL203@P4: the stacked architecture for convenience. Similarly, 4 BUs are put in vertical and 3 BUs are put in\nL204@P4: horizontal for a 4 × 3 BUG. Each BU involves a modular addition, a modular subtraction, and a\nL205@P4: twiddle factor modular multiplication. With a bit-reversed polynomial as input if the dataflow goes\nL206@P4: from left to right, NTT is done. The Inverse NTT (INTT) is computed in an inverse dataflow: it\nL207@P4: accepts coefficients in normal order and produces outputs in bit-reversed order. We use 𝑁𝑇𝑇 𝐷𝐼𝑇\nL208@P4: 𝑅→𝑁\nL209@P4: and 𝐼𝑁𝑇𝑇 𝐷𝐼𝑇\nL210@P4: 𝑁→𝑅\nL211@P4: to denote these two different dataflows as what [19] does.\nL212@P4: The algorithm requires 𝑂(𝑁 log 𝑁) arithmetic operations and storage for at least 𝑁 coefficients\nL213@P4: and 𝑁\nL214@P4: 2\nL215@P4: twiddle factors, which are constants used in the transform. This makes it efficient for smaller\nL216@P4: polynomials (e.g., 𝑁 ≤ 2\nL217@P4: 14), where on-chip memory can accommodate all data. However, for large\nL218@P4: polynomials (e.g., 𝑁 ≥ 2\nL219@P4: 20) on FPGAs, the standard NTT becomes impractical because (1) the\nL220@P4: available on-chip memory cannot accommodate all coefficients and twiddle factors simultaneously,\nL221@P4: and (2) operating on wide (e.g., log2(𝑞) = 256) integers substantially increases the cost of modular\nL222@P4: arithmetic, thereby straining FPGA resources.\nL223@P4: To address the memory and computation constraints of standard NTT for large-size polynomials,\nL224@P4: the four-step NTT algorithm leverages the mathematical structure of NTT by decomposing an\nL225@P4: 𝑁-point transform into four phases. As shown in Figure 3, suppose a 1D polynomial 𝑁 can be\nL226@P4: decomposed into a 2D matrix 𝑁 = 𝑛1 × 𝑛2, the steps are as follows:\nL227@P4: (1) Column-Wise NTTs. Compute 𝑛1-size NTT on all the 𝑛2 columns.\nL228@P4: (2) Twiddle Factor Multiplication. Multiply the matrix with the corresponding twiddle factors.\nL229@P4: (3) Row-Wise NTTs. Compute 𝑛2-size NTT on all the 𝑛1 rows.\nL230@P4: (4) Final Transposition. Transpose the matrix to restore the original 1D polynomial.\nL231@P4-5: ACM Transactions on Reconfigurable Technology and Systems, Vol. 18, No. 4, Article 554:6 Q. Hu et al.\nL232@P5: Fig. 3. Four-step NTT algorithm.\nL233@P5: Table 1. Comparison with Existing Approaches for Large-Size NTT\nL234@P5: Approach\nL235@P5: NTT\nL236@P5: PE\nL237@P5: Arch.\nL238@P5: Decomposition\nL239@P5: Flexibility Parallelism\nL240@P5: Max\nL241@P5: Vector\nL242@P5: Size\nL243@P5: Data\nL244@P5: Movement\nL245@P5: Overhead\nL246@P5: PipeZK [46] Dataflow N N 8 Middle\nL247@P5: SAM [40] Dataflow Y Y 32 High\nL248@P5: CycloneNTT [1] Stacked N/A N 64 High\nL249@P5: HiFA (this article) Stacked Y Y 64 Middle\nL250@P5: 𝑌 = Yes, 𝑁 = No, 𝑁 /𝐴 = Not Applicable.\nL251@P5: 2.2 Motivation of HiFA\nL252@P5: Four-step NTT reduces data dependency complexity compared to the standard NTT algorithm. It\nL253@P5: also enables the parallelism among those sub-polynomial and reduces the number or complexity of\nL254@P5: twiddle factors needed within the small NTT PEs. While four-step NTT supports the large-size NTTs\nL255@P5: and reduces on-chip memory demands, it also brings extra challenges. (1) It doubles the number of\nL256@P5: NTT computations (row-wise and column-wise), amplifying computation overhead. (2) It introduces\nL257@P5: extra data movement that requires non-sequential access to off-chip HBM during the matrix\nL258@P5: transpose phase, leading to inefficient memory bandwidth utilization and thus increased delays.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · NVIDIA/cuPQC · GitHub (https://github.com/NVIDIA/cuPQC/releases)\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/NVIDIA/cuPQC/releases\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 143\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: \nL5: Appearance settings\nL6: \nL7:   * Platform\nL8: \nL9:     * AI CODE CREATION\nL10:       * 【2†GitHub CopilotWrite better code with AI】\nL11:       * 【3†GitHub Copilot appDirect agents from issue to merge】\nL12:       * 【4†MCP RegistryIntegrate external tools】\nL13: \nL14:     * DEVELOPER WORKFLOWS\nL15:       * 【5†ActionsAutomate any workflow】\nL16:       * 【6†Cod